--- expect
error E_NOT_NUM at 1:1
===

%
% --- int(i) — E_NOT_INT
%
% A whole number was required and a fraction was given. Had no coverage at all
% before this file.
### name: mis.not-int.substr-start
--- source
SUBSTR("abc", 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:15
===
### name: mis.not-int.find-from
--- source
FIND("a", "abc", 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:18
===
### name: mis.not-int.char
--- source
CHAR(1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:6
===
### name: mis.not-int.left
--- note
A fractional count is E_NOT_INT, distinct from E_RANGE: the value is the wrong
shape rather than out of bounds.
--- source
LEFT("abc", 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:13
===
### name: mis.not-int.right
--- source
RIGHT("abc", 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:14
===
### name: mis.not-int.substr-len
--- source
SUBSTR("abc", 1, 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:18
===
### name: mis.not-int.repeat
--- source
REPEAT("a", 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:13
===
### name: mis.not-int.padl
--- source
PADL("a", 1.5, "x")
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:11
===
### name: mis.not-int.padr
--- source
PADR("a", 1.5, "x")
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:11
===
### name: mis.not-int.round-scale
--- source
ROUND(1, 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:10
===
### name: mis.not-int.power-exp
--- source
POWER(2, 1.5)
--- expect
error E_NOT_INT at 1:10
===

%
% --- nonNegInt(i) — E_RANGE
%
% Same accessor as above, one step further: an integer that is negative.
### name: mis.range.left
--- note
A negative count is E_RANGE, not a silent clamp to zero. nonNegInt() raises it
against the argument's own position.
--- source
LEFT("abc", -1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:13
===
### name: mis.range.right
--- source
RIGHT("abc", -1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:14
===
### name: mis.range.substr-len
--- source
SUBSTR("abc", 1, -1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:18
===
### name: mis.range.repeat
--- source
REPEAT("a", -1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:13
===
### name: mis.range.padl
--- source
PADL("a", -1, "x")
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:11
===
### name: mis.range.padr
--- source
PADR("a", -1, "x")
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:11
===
### name: mis.range.round-scale
--- source
ROUND(1, -1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:10
===
### name: mis.range.power-exp
--- source
POWER(2, -1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:10
===

%
% --- symbol(i) — E_EXPECT_SYMBOL
%
% The three-argument aggregate form takes a bare identifier, checked by looking at
% the AST node rather than at a value.
### name: mis.symbol.all-num
--- source
ALL((1), 1, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:10
===
### name: mis.symbol.all-grouped
--- note
(X) is a grouped expression, not a bare name. The binder form is an AST shape
check, and parenthesising it defeats the check rather than satisfying it.
--- source
ALL((1), (X), TRUE)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:11
===
### name: mis.symbol.any
--- source
ANY((1), 1, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:10
===
### name: mis.symbol.map
--- source
MAP((1), 1, _)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:10
===
### name: mis.symbol.filter
--- source
FILTER((1), 1, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:13
===
### name: mis.symbol.sum
--- source
SUM((1), 1, _)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:10
===
### name: mis.symbol.all-text
--- source
ALL((1), "X", TRUE)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:10
===

%
% --- values outside their permitted range — E_RANGE
%
### name: mis.domain.char-negative
--- source
CHAR(-1)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:6
===
### name: mis.domain.ltb-too-big
--- source
LTB((256))
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:6
===
### name: mis.domain.ltb-negative
--- source
LTB((-1))
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:6
===
### name: mis.domain.ltb-fraction
--- source
LTB((1.5))
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:6
===
### name: mis.domain.round-scale-cap
--- source
ROUND(1.5, 1000001)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:12
===
### name: mis.domain.power-cap
--- source
POWER(2, 100001)
--- expect
error E_RANGE at 1:10
===

%
% --- well-typed but unusable — E_BAD_ARG
%
### name: mis.badarg.base64-bad
--- source
DECODE_BASE64("!!!!")
--- expect
error E_BAD_ARG at 1:15
===

%
% --- value-level failures
%
### name: mis.value.mod-zero
--- source
1 % 0
--- expect
error E_DIV_ZERO at 1:3
===
### name: mis.value.band-mismatch
--- source
FROM_HEX("00") BAND FROM_HEX("0102")
--- expect
error E_LEN_MISMATCH at 1:16
===
### name: mis.value.bxor-mismatch
--- source
FROM_HEX("00") BXOR FROM_HEX("0102")
--- expect
error E_LEN_MISMATCH at 1:16
===
### name: mis.value.from-utf8-invalid
--- source
FROM_UTF8(FROM_HEX("ff"))
--- expect
error E_UTF8 at 1:11
===
### name: mis.value.from-utf8-truncated
--- note
A truncated multi-byte sequence is invalid UTF-8, not a partial character to be
salvaged.
--- source
FROM_UTF8(FROM_HEX("e28c"))
--- expect
error E_UTF8 at 1:11
===

%
% --- lookup failures
%
### name: mis.lookup.undef-var
--- source
NOPE_VAR
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:1
===
### name: mis.lookup.no-key
--- source
A = 1; A["nope"]
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY at 1:9
===
### name: mis.lookup.no-scalar
--- note
FILTER returns a childless NONE when nothing matched. Scalar context on it has
nothing to reach for, which is E_NO_SCALAR rather than an empty string.
--- source
FILTER((1, 2), _ > 5) + 1
--- expect
error E_NO_SCALAR at 1:1
===

%
% --- reserved words — E_RESERVED
%
% Each of the eleven reserved words, as a variable and as an aggregate binder.
%
% Six of these do NOT raise E_RESERVED, and that is the point of having them:
% three of the eleven never reach the reserved-word check at all. NOT is a
% prefix operator, so `NOT = 1` fails in the grammar first; TRUE and FALSE lex
% as boolean literals rather than identifiers, so they fail the assignment
% target check or the binder shape check instead. The template that generated
% this section assumed one code for all eleven; the words that break the
% assumption are worth pinning precisely because they break it.
### name: mis.reserved.var-and
--- source
AND = 1
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-and
--- note
A reserved word is rejected as an aggregate binder for the same reason it is
rejected as a variable — the binder introduces a name.
--- source
ALL((1), AND, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-or
--- source
OR = 1
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-or
--- source
ALL((1), OR, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-not
--- note
NOT is a prefix operator, so this fails in the grammar
before any reserved-word check runs.
--- source
NOT = 1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:5
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-not
--- note
Same: the binder position is parsed as an expression,
and NOT there is a prefix operator with nothing to apply.
--- source
ALL((1), NOT, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:13
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-xor
--- source
XOR = 1
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-xor
--- source
ALL((1), XOR, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-eql
--- source
EQL = 1
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-eql
--- source
ALL((1), EQL, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-in
--- source
IN = 1
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-in
--- source
ALL((1), IN, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-band
--- source
BAND = 1
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-band
--- source
ALL((1), BAND, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-bor
--- source
BOR = 1
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-bor
--- source
ALL((1), BOR, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-bxor
--- source
BXOR = 1
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-bxor
--- source
ALL((1), BXOR, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-true
--- note
TRUE lexes as a boolean literal, not an identifier, so
the assignment target check rejects it before the reserved-word check.
--- source
TRUE = 1
--- expect
error E_BAD_ASSIGN at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-true
--- note
TRUE is a boolean literal, so the binder is not a bare
name — E_EXPECT_SYMBOL, not E_RESERVED.
--- source
ALL((1), TRUE, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:10
===
### name: mis.reserved.var-false
--- note
As var-true.
--- source
FALSE = 1
--- expect
error E_BAD_ASSIGN at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.binder-false
--- note
As binder-true.
--- source
ALL((1), FALSE, TRUE)
--- expect
error E_EXPECT_SYMBOL at 1:10
===

%
% --- assignment targets — E_BAD_ASSIGN
%
### name: mis.badassign.literal-target
--- source
1 = 2
--- expect
error E_BAD_ASSIGN at 1:1
===
### name: mis.badassign.call-target
--- source
LEN("a") = 1
--- expect
error E_BAD_ASSIGN at 1:1
===
### name: mis.badassign.grouped-target
--- note
(A) = 1 is rejected even though A = 1 is fine. The target must be an identifier
followed by index operations, and grouping makes it an expression.
--- source
(A) = 1
--- expect
error E_BAD_ASSIGN at 1:2
===
### name: mis.badassign.compound-literal
--- source
1 += 2
--- expect
error E_BAD_ASSIGN at 1:1
===

