% --- malformed token streams — E_SYNTAX
%
### name: mis.syntax.trailing-operator
--- source
1 +
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:4
===
### name: mis.syntax.unclosed-paren
--- source
(1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:3
===
### name: mis.syntax.unclosed-call
--- source
LEN(1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:6
===
### name: mis.syntax.unclosed-bracket
--- source
A[1
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:4
===
### name: mis.syntax.stray-close
--- source
1)
--- expect
error E_SYNTAX at 1:2
===

%
% --- compile-time name resolution
%
### name: mis.compile.unknown-func-lower
--- source
nosuch(1)
--- expect
error E_UNKNOWN_FUNC at 1:1
===
### name: mis.reserved.not-in-a-tight-position
--- note
NOT is a prefix operator at binding power 7, looser than comparison at 8, so it
cannot appear as a comparison operand. The parser falls through to the primary
rule, which sees a bare reserved word.
--- setup
A = TRUE; B = TRUE
--- source
A == NOT B
--- expect
error E_RESERVED at 1:6
===
### name: mis.reserved.not-with-parens-is-read-as-a-call
--- note
The same expression with parentheses is a different error, because the primary
rule tries the call lookahead -- an identifier followed by "(" -- before it
rejects a reserved word. NOT( therefore looks like a call to a function named
NOT, and that is E_UNKNOWN_FUNC rather than E_RESERVED.

Nothing pinned this before. It is not obviously the right answer and it is not
written down anywhere, which is exactly why it is pinned now: all six hosts agree
on it today, and the parser rewrites have no business changing it by accident.
The branch order in the primary rule is what decides it, so a host that reorders
those two checks would silently swap the code.
--- setup
A = TRUE; B = TRUE
--- source
A == NOT(B)
--- expect
error E_UNKNOWN_FUNC at 1:6
===
### name: mis.reserved.not-with-parens-where-the-prefix-is-allowed
--- note
Where NOT *is* allowed as a prefix it wins over the call reading, because the
prefix rule is consulted first and only falls through when its binding power
does not reach. So the parenthesised form is an ordinary negation here.
--- setup
B = TRUE
--- source
NOT(B)
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: mis.reserved.not-with-parens-after-unary-minus
--- note
Unary minus binds at 15, tighter than NOT at 7, so its operand is another tight
position and the call reading applies there too.
--- setup
B = TRUE
--- source
-NOT(B)
--- expect
error E_UNKNOWN_FUNC at 1:2
===
